g the pods properly. \n\nBut since the user tried to get logs for a non-existent pod, the next logical step is to list all pods to find the correct names. Then, check the deployments and services related to user-service. If the pod isn\'t there, maybe the deployment is not running, or there\'s a typo in the service name. Also, verifying the DNS resolution for prometheus-server might be necessary, but that\'s a separate issue. \n\nSo, the best next tool is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get pods -n social-network" to list all pods and see if user-service is present. If not, check deployments and services. Then, if the service is missing, check the service definitions. This approach systematically identifies where the problem lies.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pods -n social-network"  \nTo identify whether the `user-service` pod exists and is running, I need to list all pods in the `social-network` namespace. The previous error indicated the pod name might be invalid or the service isn\'t deployed. This command will confirm the current state of pods and help identify any missing or unhealthy components.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 602, 'prompt_tokens': 2020, 'total_tokens': 2622, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdcb-9e0a-72f2-b555-78407a14bf17-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2020, 'output_tokens': 602, 'total_tokens': 2622}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5d2fe841-f231-46df-918d-a9a120ac8c79'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with their social network application, which consists of many microservices. The initial steps involved checking metrics for the user-service, but that failed due to a DNS error. Then, they tried to get logs for a specific pod, but it wasn\'t found. Now, they\'re asking to list all pods in the social-network namespace to check if the service exists.\n\nFirst, I need to figure out why the previous commands didn\'t work. The get_metrics query failed because the Prometheus server wasn\'t resolvable, which could be a network issue or the service isn\'t running. Then, when trying to get logs for a specific pod, it wasn\'t found, so the pod might not be running or the name is incorrect. \n\nThe next logical step is to list all the pods in the namespace to see if the user-service pod is present. Using kubectl get pods -n social-network will show the current state of all pods. If the pod isn\'t listed, that means the service might not be deployed correctly, or there\'s a deployment issue. \n\nIf the pod isn\'t there, maybe the deployment is not functioning, or there\'s a problem with the deployment configuration. Alternatively, if the pod is in a CrashLoopBackOff state, that indicates an application error. Checking the pods will help determine if the service is even running. \n\nSo, the correct tool here is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pods -n social-network". This will provide the necessary information to proceed further. If the pod isn\'t present, the next step might be to check deployments or check the service configuration. If the pod is present but in a bad state, logs or describe commands could be used next.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-aceea521a5b8a43d', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 393, 'prompt_token