ried to get metrics for user-service, which failed. Then they checked the pods and found user-service is running. But maybe the problem is not with the pod itself but with the metrics endpoint. Since the get_metrics query failed with a DNS error, maybe the Prometheus server isn\'t accessible. But the user tried to get the metrics for user-service, which might not be the right approach. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user-service isn\'t exposed correctly, or the metrics endpoint isn\'t set up. But the pod is running. So perhaps the next step is to check the service definitions for user-service to see if it\'s properly configured to expose metrics. Or check if the Prometheus server is up and running. But the user can\'t access Prometheus directly. \n\nWait, the get_services tool was used initially, but the user didn\'t get the list of services. Maybe they should check the services in the namespace to see if the user-service has a corresponding service. Also, the get_dependency_graph could show if there are any issues in the service dependencies. Alternatively, checking the logs of the user-service pod might reveal if there are any errors related to metrics or service discovery. \n\nBut the user already tried to get the logs for a specific pod and got a not found error. Then they listed all pods and found user-service is running. So maybe the next step is to check the service associated with user-service. Using kubectl to get the service details for user-service to see if it\'s correctly configured. But the available tools don\'t include a direct way to get service details. However, the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can be used to run \'kubectl get service user-service -n social-network\' to check the service configuration. \n\nAlternatively, using get_metrics again with a different query. But the initial error was DNS resolution. Maybe the Prometheus server isn\'t reachable. But the user can\'t check that directly. Another approach is to check the service dependencies using get_dependency_graph to see if there are any issues in the network communication. \n\nWait, the user\'s main goal is to find the root cause of the faults. Since the initial metrics query failed, and the pod is running, maybe the problem is that the user-service isn\'t exposing metrics, or the Prometheus server isn\'t properly configured. But without access to Prometheus, it\'s hard to tell. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user-service isn\'t part of the services listed in Grafana. Using get_services to get the list of services in Grafana might help. But the user tried get_services initially, but the response wasn\'t provided. Wait, the first tool call was get_services, but the user didn\'t show the response. Wait, looking back, the user\'s first tool call was get_services, but the response wasn\'t given. Wait, the user\'s first step was to call get_services, but the assistant\'s response was to call get_metrics, which failed. Then they tried to check the pods. \n\nWait, maybe the user should first get the list of services from Grafana using get_services to see if user-service is listed. If it\'s not, then maybe the service isn\'t properly registered. Alternatively, check the service dependencies to see if there are any issues. \n\nBut given the current state, the user has confirmed that the user-service pod is running. The next step is to check if the service for user-service is correctly set up. So using exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the service details. So the command would be \'kubectl get service user-service -n social-network\'. That would show if the service exists and its endpoints. If the service isn\'t created, that\'s a problem. \n\nAlternatively, check the deployment for user-service to see if it\'s correctly configured. Using \'kubectl get deployment user-service -n social-network\'. But the user might not have the deployment name. \n\nAlternatively, check the logs of the user-service pod for any errors related to metrics or service discovery. But the user tried to get logs for a specific pod and got a not found error, but after listing all pods, they found the correct pod name. So maybe they should try getting the logs again with the correct pod name. \n\nWait, the user previously tried to get logs for \'user-service-7df5d686d6-25x9k\' which wasn\'t found, but the current list shows \'user-service-559b79c875-hjhqb\' is running. So the correct pod name is that one. So the next step is to get the logs for that pod to check for any errors. \n\nSo the tool call would be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command \'kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-hjhqb -n social-network\'. That would show the logs and help identify if there\'s an issue with the service or metrics. \n\nAlternatively, check the service endpoints to see if the user-service is properly exposed. So using \'kubectl get endpoints user-service -n social-network\'. \n\nBut given the available tools, the user can use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to run these commands. So the next step is to check the logs of the user-service pod to see if there are any errors that could explain the metrics issue. That would be the most direct way to find out if the service is functioning correctly.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-hjhqb -n social-network"  \nThe `user-service` pod is listed as running, but the earlier metrics query failed with a DNS error. To investigate potential issues within the pod itself (e.g., misconfigured service discovery, missing metrics endpoints, or internal errors), I will retrieve its logs. This will help identify if the pod is generating errors or misconfigurations that could explain the failed metrics query or other faults.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1301, 'prompt_tokens': 3181, 'total_tokens': 4482, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'o