figured.\n\nAnother angle: the logs show the pod is running, but the MongoDB connection is failing. So the issue is likely with the MongoDB service itself. Let me check the user-mongodb pods first. If they\'re not running, that\'s the root cause. If they are running, maybe the service isn\'t properly set up, or there\'s a port issue. Also, checking the MongoDB pods\' logs could reveal if the service is starting correctly and listening on the right port.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pods -n social-network | grep user-mongodb"  \nThe logs indicate the `user-service` pod is failing to connect to `user-mongodb`. To confirm if the `user-mongodb` service is running and healthy, I need to check the status of its associated pods. If the MongoDB pods are not running or are in a crash loop, that would explain the connection failures. This command will filter the list of pods to focus on `user-mongodb`-related components.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 437, 'prompt_tokens': 5421, 'total_tokens': 5858, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdcc-24f9-7272-bf3f-5e5c82b151c8-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5421, 'output_tokens': 437, 'total_tokens': 5858}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='4dc72919-27e4-4bcb-adb9-59460287a244'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user-service logs show repeated errors connecting to user-mongodb. The next step is to check if the user-mongodb pods are running properly. I should use kubectl to get the pods in the social-network namespace and look for any issues with the user-mongodb service. The command would be to list all pods and then grep for user-mongodb. That way, I can see if the MongoDB pods are up and running or if there's a problem with them. If they're not running, that's the root cause. If they are, maybe there's a network issue or configuration problem. Let me generate the tool call for that.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n social-network | grep user-mongodb"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ad002c3ac54faa37', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 174, 'prompt_tokens': 6434, 'total_tokens': 6608, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdcc-3a4f-76f2-8b77-cd04ce8a171e-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n social-network | grep user-mongodb'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ad002c3ac54faa37', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6434, 'output_tokens': 174, 'total_tokens': 6608}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected (ValueError): Pipe commands are forbidden', id='df66c3b2-48ea-4df9-80f9-2b8549ef43da', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-ad002c3ac54faa37'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9cfe04f2-f519-4716-9ea0-c97e2d1fd2aa'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user tried to run a command with a pipe, but it\'s not allowed. The available tools don\'t include a way to process pipes, so I need to adjust. The goal is to check the status of user-mongodb pods. Instead of using grep in the command, I should use a different approach. Maybe use kubectl get