ng the pods properly. \n\nBut since the user tried to get logs for a non-existent pod, the next logical step is to list all pods to find the correct names. Then, check the deployments and services related to user-service. If the pod isn\'t there, maybe the deployment is not running, or there\'s a typo in the service name. Also, verifying the DNS resolution for prometheus-server might be necessary, but that\'s a separate issue. \n\nSo, the best next tool is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get pods -n social-network" to list all pods and see if user-service is present. If not, check deployments and services. Then, if the service is missing, check the service definitions. This approach systematically identifies where the problem lies.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pods -n social-network"  \nTo identify whether the `user-service` pod exists and is running, I need to list all pods in the `social-network` namespace. The previous error indicated the pod name might be invalid or the service isn\'t deployed. This command will confirm the current state of pods and help identify any missing or unhealthy components.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 602, 'prompt_tokens': 2020, 'total_tokens': 2622, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdcb-9e0a-72f2-b555-78407a14bf17-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2020, 'output_tokens': 602, 'total_tokens': 2622}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5d2fe841-f231-46df-918d-a9a120ac8c79'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with their social network applica