hout access to Prometheus, it\'s hard to tell. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user-service isn\'t part of the services listed in Grafana. Using get_services to get the list of services in Grafana might help. But the user tried get_services initially, but the response wasn\'t provided. Wait, the first tool call was get_services, but the user didn\'t show the response. Wait, looking back, the user\'s first tool call was get_services, but the response wasn\'t given. Wait, the user\'s first step was to call get_services, but the assistant\'s response was to call get_metrics, which failed. Then they tried to check the pods. \n\nWait, maybe the user should first get the list of services from Grafana using get_services to see if user-service is listed. If it\'s not, then maybe the service isn\'t properly registered. Alternatively, check the service dependencies to see if there are any issues. \n\nBut given the current state, the user has confirmed that the user-service pod is running. The next step is to check if the service for user-service is correctly set up. So using exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the service details. So the command would be \'kubectl get service user-service -n social-network\'. That would show if the service exists and its endpoints. If the service isn\'t created, that\'s a problem. \n\nAlternatively, check the deployment for user-service to see if it\'s correctly configured. Using \'kubectl get deployment user-service -n social-network\'. But the user might not have the deployment name. \n\nAlternatively, check the logs of the user-service pod for any errors related to metrics or service discovery. But the user tried to get logs for a specific pod and got a not found error, but after listing all pods, they found the correct pod name. So maybe they should try getting the logs again with the correct pod name. \n\nWait, the user previously tried to get logs for \'user-service-7df5d686d6-25x9k\' which wasn\'t found, but the current list shows \'user-service-559b79c875-hjhqb\' is running. So the correct pod name is that one. So the next step is to get the logs for that pod to check for any errors. \n\nSo the tool call would be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command \'kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-hjhqb -n social-network\'. That would show the logs and help identify if there\'s an issue with the service or metrics. \n\nAlternatively, check the service endpoints to see if the user-service is properly exposed. So using \'kubectl get endpoints user-service -n social-network\'. \n\nBut given the available tools, the user can use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to run these commands. So the next step is to check the logs of the user-service pod to see if there are any errors that could explain the metrics issue. That would be the most direct way to find out if the service is functioning correctly.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-hjhqb -n social-network"  \nThe `user-service` pod is listed as running, but the earlier metrics query failed with a DNS error. To investigate potential issues within the pod itself (e.g., misconfigured service discovery, missing metrics endpoints, or internal errors), I will retrieve its logs. This will help identify if the pod is generating errors or misconfigurations that could explain the failed metrics query or other faults.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1301, 'prompt_tokens': 3181, 'total_tokens': 4482, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdcb-cf16-7950-b401-2ecb47702396-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3181, 'output_tokens': 1301, 'total_tokens': 4482}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='cc8f1c8e-8606-4ad0-b378-a22b620aad06'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with their social network application, which consists of many microservices. The initial problem was that when they tried to get metrics for the user-service, there was a DNS error resolving prometheus-server.observe.svc.cluster.local. \n\nFirst, they used get_services to list all services in Grafana, but that didn't directly help. Then they tried get_metrics with a PromQL query, which failed due to DNS. Next, they checked the pods in the social-network namespace using kubectl get pods, and found that user-service is running. But when they tried to get logs for a specific pod, the pod name was incorrect. They then corrected the pod name and tried again.\n\nNow, the user is in the thinking stage again. The last step was attempting to get logs for user-service-559b79c875-hjhqb, which is the correct pod name. The response from the tool call would be the logs of that pod. The next step is to analyze those logs to find out why the DNS resolution failed. \n\nPossible issues could be misconfigured service discovery in the user-service, such as incorrect Prometheus server address. Alternatively, the pod might not be exporting metrics properly, leading to the up{} metric not being reported. Another angle is network policies blocking the pod from reaching the Prometheus server. \n\nThe user should check the logs for any errors related to DNS resolution or service discovery. If the logs show that the service is trying to connect to the wrong Prometheus endpoint, that's a configuration issue. If there are no logs, maybe the pod isn't configured to expose metrics, or the metrics endpoint is down. \n\nSince the DNS error is specific to the user-service, looking into its logs and configuration (like environment variables or config maps) would be crucial. They might also need to check the service definitions for user-service to ensure they're correctly pointing to Prometheus. \n\nThe next tool call should be to get the logs of the user-service pod to inspect for any errors or misconfigurations that could explain the DNS resolution failure and the missing metrics.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-hjhqb -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-be72023842819deb', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 482, 'prompt_tokens': 4196, 'total_tokens': 4678, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdcc-0b8c-7332-a66b-4e3fd1809777-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs user-service-559b79c875-hjhqb -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-be72023842819deb', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4196, 'output_tokens': 482, 'total_tokens': 4678}), ToolMessage(content="[2026-Mar-17 21:52:37.887617] <info>: (UserHandler.h:932:GetMachineId) MAC address = 0a:54:4f:39:17:7f\n[2026-Mar-17 21:52:37.887886] <info>: (UserService.cpp:63:main) machine_id = 316\n[2026-Mar-17 21:52:38.189174] <error>: (utils_mongodb.h:76:CreateIndex) Error in createIndexes: No suitable servers found: `serverSelectionTimeoutMS` expired: [unknown connection error calling ismaster on 'user-mongodb:27017']\n[2026-Mar-17 21:52:38.189354] <error>: (UserService.cpp:80:main) Failed to create mongodb index, try again\n[2026-Mar-17 21:52:39.489156] <error>: (utils_mongodb.h:76:CreateIndex) Error in createIndexes: No suitable servers found: `serverSelectionTimeoutMS` expired: [unknown connection error calling ismaster on 'u