, oldest timestamp: (0, 0) , meta checkpoint timestamp: (0, 0)"}}\n', id='29720afb-8ee2-4a4f-bdd9-25354ce01823', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-86a5d438bdb77e60'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b05a6d57-c154-421a-a183-9285e4abfd16'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to fix the issue where the user-service can\'t connect to user-mongodb. Earlier steps showed that the service\'s targetPort was correctly set to 27017, and the pod\'s logs don\'t show immediate errors. But the endpoints are still missing. \n\nHmm, maybe the pod isn\'t actually running or is in a CrashLoopBackOff state. Wait, the logs I saw earlier were from the MongoDB pod, and they seemed to be normal index creation and checkpoint messages. No obvious errors there. So why are the endpoints not showing up?\n\nWait, the user mentioned that the service\'s ENDPOINTS are <none>, but when they ran \'kubectl get endpoints\', there was an IP listed. That\'s conflicting. Maybe the service is misconfigured in another way. Let me check the service\'s YAML again. Oh, right, the user previously tried patching the service to set the port, but it said "no change". Maybe the original service YAML didn\'t have the port specified, so the patch didn\'t apply because it was already set. Wait, no—the initial get service command might have shown the port was missing. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the pod isn\'t labeled correctly. Wait, the user checked the pod\'s labels and they match the service\'s selector. So that\'s not the issue. \n\nWait, maybe the pod isn\'t running. Let me check the pod\'s status. The user ran \'kubectl describe pod\' earlier, but maybe the pod is in a pending state due to resource limits or node issues. Or maybe the pod is crashing but the logs aren\'t showing it. \n\nWait, the logs from the pod show some index creation and checkpoint activities, which are normal. So the pod is running, but maybe the service isn\'t properly exposing it. Or perhaps there\'s a network policy blocking the traffic. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the MongoDB pod isn\'t listening on the correct port. Wait, the logs show that indexes were created, which implies the MongoDB service is running. But if the service isn\'t properly configured, the endpoints won\'t be created. \n\nWait, the user tried to patch the service to set the port, but it said "no change". That suggests that the port was already set. So why are the endpoints still missing? Maybe the service is using a different port than what\'s expected. \n\nWait, maybe the service is of type ClusterIP, which is the default, but the cluster isn\'t routing traffic correctly. Or maybe there\'s a typo in the service name. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the MongoDB pod isn\'t ready yet. Let me check the pod\'s readiness status. If the pod is in a CrashLoopBackOff state, it might not be ready. But the logs don\'t show any errors. \n\nWait, the user-service is trying to connect to user-mongodb, which is failing. The service\'s endpoints are missing, so the user-service can\'t resolve the service name to an IP. So the root cause is that the service isn\'t properly pointing to the pod. \n\nWait, maybe the service\'s selector is incorrect. Let me check the service\'s YAML again. The user-service is trying to connect to user-mongodb, so the service\'s selector should match the pod\'s labels. The user previously checked that the labels match, so that\'s not the issue. \n\nHmm, maybe the service is not properly configured with the correct port. The user-service might be trying to connect to a different port than what\'s exposed by the service. For example, if the service is exposing port 27017 but the pod is listening on a different port, like 30000. \n\nWait, the user checked the pod\'s YAML and found that the container\'s port is 27017, which matches the service\'s targetPort. So that\'s correct. \n\nWait, maybe the MongoDB pod isn\'t actually running. Let me check the pod\'s status again. If the pod is in a CrashLoopBackOff state, it\'s crashing repeatedly. But the logs don\'t show any errors. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the MongoDB image is incorrect or there\'s a configuration error in the pod\'s YAML. For example, if the MongoDB container isn\'t starting because of a missing volume or configuration file. \n\nWait, the user hasn\'t checked the pod\'s YAML for any other issues. Let me check the pod\'s YAML again. The user ran \'kubectl get pod -n social-network -l app=user-mongodb -o yaml\' earlier. If that command shows any issues, like missing volumes or incorrect image tags, that could be the problem. \n\nBut the user hasn\'t provided the output of that command. Wait, in the previous step, the user ran \'kubectl get pod -n social-network -l app=user-mongodb -o yaml\' and the response was the pod\'s YAML. Let me check that again. \n\nWait, the user\'s last tool call was to get the pod\'s logs, which showed some normal MongoDB activities. But the pod\'s YAML might have issues. For example, if the container\'s port is set to 27017 but the service\'s targetPort is different. \n\nWait, the user already confirmed that the service\'s targetPort is 27017, and the pod\'s container port is also 27017. So that\'s correct. \n\nHmm, maybe the issue is with the service\'s type. If the service is of type ClusterIP, but the cluster isn\'t assigning an IP, or if there\'s a misconfiguration in the network policies. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the MongoDB pod isn\'t ready yet, and the endpoints aren\'t updated until the pod is ready. Let me check the pod\'s status. If the pod is in a Pending state, that\'s a problem. \n\nWait, the user hasn\'t checked the pod\'s status yet. Let me run \'kubectl get pod -n social-network -l app=user-mongodb\' to see the pod\'s status. If the pod is in a CrashLoopBackOff state, that\'s a problem. \n\nBut the user hasn\'t done that yet. So the next step is to check the pod\'s status and any associated events to see why it\'s not running properly. \n\nSo, the next tool to use is \'kubectl get pod -n social-network -l app=user-mongodb\' to check the pod\'s status and events. That will help determine if the pod is crashing or if there\'s another issue.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\nreasoning: The MongoDB pod\'s logs show normal index creation and checkpoint activities, but endpoints are still missing. This suggests the pod may be in a CrashLoopBackOff state or the service is misconfigured. I need to verify the pod\'s status and events to determine if it\'s crashing or if there\'s a deeper issue (e.g., resource limits, image errors). \n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get pod -n social-network -l app=user-mongodb -o jsonpath=\'{.items[0].status.phase}\'}"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 1457, 'prompt_tokens': 4509, 'total_tokens': 5966, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdcf-9517-76e0-9f29-fab76ccd7643-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4509, 'output_tokens': 1457, 'total_tokens': 5966}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='3be01611-c14b-4457-ae9e-5ac992de0c57'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the user-mongodb service isn\'t working. They checked the service\'s configuration, the pod\'s YAML, the logs, and the pod\'s status. The service\'s targetPort matches the pod\'s container port, and the logs don\'t show any critical errors. But the endpoints are still missing, which is causing the connection issues.\n\nThe last step was checking the pod\'s phase with the command "kubectl get pod -n social-network -l app=user-mongodb -o jsonpath=\'{.items[0].status.phase}\'". The response from the tool call would give the pod\'s current state. If the phase is "Running", then the pod is up, but maybe there\'s a network policy or firewall rule blocking the connection. If the phase is something else like "Pending" or "Error", that points to a different issue.\n\nAssuming the pod is running, the next step would be to check the network policies or ensure that the service is correctly exposed. Alternatively, maybe the MongoDB instance isn\'t listening on the correct port or there\'s a configuration issue within the MongoDB container. Another possibility is that the service is correctly configured, but the pods aren\'t being scheduled properly due to resource constraints or node issues.\n\nSince the user has already checked the service and pod configurations, the next logical step is to verify the pod\'s status. If the pod is running, then the problem might be with the service\'s exposure or network policies. If the pod isn\'t running, they need to investigate why, maybe looking into the pod\'s events or logs for more details. But given the previous logs didn\'t show errors, it\'s possible the pod is running but the service isn\'t properly routing traffic. So, checking the pod\'s phase is crucial to determine the next steps.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pod -n social-network -l app=user-mongodb -o jsonpath=\'{.items[0].status.phase}\'"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-97279b4f4483ce04', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 423, 'prompt_tokens': 5656, 'total_tokens': 6079, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'log