alls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get namespaces"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-833bcc1ef0d4a675', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 344, 'prompt_tokens': 4305, 'total_tokens': 4649, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdd1-e9d7-78d1-bffe-889a1ba65dc7-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get namespaces'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-833bcc1ef0d4a675', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4305, 'output_tokens': 344, 'total_tokens': 4649}), ToolMessage(content='NAME              STATUS        AGE\nastronomy-shop    Terminating   44m\ndefault           Active        20h\nkube-flannel      Active        20h\nkube-node-lease   Active        20h\nkube-public       Active        20h\nkube-system       Active        20h\nobserve           Active        48m\nopenebs           Active        48m\nsregym            Active        45m\n', id='c95f64a9-a11b-4dbc-b1d8-382a2b49cb9f', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-833bcc1ef0d4a675'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d3ffd0dd-eb94-43d0-a4c5-fce6c7818a0c'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to troubleshoot the user-mongodb service in the social-network application. Earlier, they tried to get the service YAML in the social-network namespace, but that namespace doesn't exist. Then they checked all namespaces and found that the relevant one might be 'observe' or others, but the application's namespace wasn't listed. Wait, the user mentioned the application belongs to the 'social-network' namespace, but that's not present. Maybe there's a typo or a different namespace name.\n\nWait, looking at the namespaces listed: there's 'observe', 'astronomy-shop', 'sregym', etc. The user's app is supposed to be in 'social-network', but that's not there. Maybe the correct namespace is 'observe'? Or perhaps the user made a mistake in the namespace name. Alternatively, maybe the application is deployed in a different namespace that wasn't listed. Wait, the output shows 'observe' as an active namespace. Maybe the user intended to use 'observe' instead of 'social-network'? \n\nThe next step is to check if the service exists in any of the existing namespaces. The user needs to find the correct namespace where the social-network application is deployed. Since 'social-network' isn't present, perhaps the actual namespace is 'observe' or another one. To confirm, the user should check each relevant namespace for the service. Alternatively, maybe the namespace was created with a different name, like 'social-network-ns' or similar. \n\nBut the user's initial information said the app is in 'social-network', which doesn't exist. So the first action is to verify the correct namespace. The next tool call should be to check the services in each namespace to see where user-mongodb is located. Alternatively, check the services in the 'observe' namespace, which is present. So, using 'kubectl get services -n observe' would help identify if the service exists there. That makes sense. So the tool to use is exec_kubectl_cmd_safely with the command to get services in the 'observe' namespace. That way, the user can confirm if the service is there and proceed accordingly.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\nJustification: The `social-network` namespace is missing, so we need to check if the application is deployed in an existing namespace like `observe` (which appears in the namespace list). This step will confirm whether the `user-mongodb` service exists in a valid namespace and identify the correct context for further troubleshooting.\n\nNext step: Generate a tool call to check services in the `observe` namespace.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 539, 'prompt_tokens': 3467, 'total_tokens': 4006, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdd1-fcd4-7310-b0ce-cc62849fe5aa-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3467, 'output_tokens': 539, 'total_tokens': 4006}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='611fe74c-b533-4387-946e-d2fe6e10e277'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to troubleshoot the user-mongodb service in the social-network namespace, but that namespace doesn\'t exist. The previous step showed that the available namespaces include \'observe\', so maybe the application is actually in the \'observe\' namespace.\n\nThe next logical step is to check the services in the \'observe\' namespace to see if user-mongodb is there. Using the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool with the command "kubectl -n observe get services" would list all services in that namespace. This will help confirm if the service exists and identify any issues with its configuration, like missing endpoints or incorrect selectors. \n\nIf the service is found there, the user can then check its YAML details to ensure the port and targetPort are correctly set. If not, they might need to look into other namespaces or check if the namespace was misnamed. This approach ensures they\'re working with the correct namespace and service configuration.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl