n\nThe right tool here is `exec_kubectl_cmd_safely` to check for the pod across all namespaces. If the pod isn't present, the service can't be properly configured. So, the command would be `kubectl get pods --all-namespaces` to find any existing user-mongodb pod. If it's missing, the next step would be to create it or check the deployment. \n\nWait, but the user already checked services in all namespaces and didn't find user-mongodb. So the pod might not exist. But maybe the deployment for user-mongodb is misconfigured. Alternatively, the service might be pointing to a non-existent pod. \n\nSo, the next action is to check for the pod in all namespaces. If it's not there, the issue is with the pod not being created. If it is there, then the service configuration might be wrong. Let's proceed with checking the pods across all namespaces.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\nJustification: The `user-mongodb` service and pod are missing from all checked namespaces. To confirm whether the pod exists in any namespace, we need to scan all namespaces simultaneously using `--all-namespaces`. If the pod is absent, the issue likely stems from a deployment or configuration error, requiring further investigation into the pod's creation or service binding.\n\nNext step: Generate a tool call to list all pods across namespaces.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 531, 'prompt_tokens': 6713, 'total_tokens': 7244, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdd2-bc45-73b3-9740-dfb52fffd814-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6713, 'output_tokens': 531, 'total_tokens': 7244}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='3b4bf8a9-2000-4bf3-b197-672c01d1bf04'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is trying to resolve an issue where the user-service can't connect to user-mongodb. They've checked several namespaces and haven't found the service or pod. The last step was checking all pods across namespaces, but the response didn't show user-mongodb. \n\nHmm, maybe the pod isn't running or was never created. The user-service might be trying to connect to a service that doesn't exist. Alternatively, there could be a typo in the service name. Let me think about the tools available. The exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool can be used to check for the pod in all namespaces. Wait, they already did that. The output didn't show user-mongodb. \n\nWait, maybe the service is misconfigured. If the service isn't properly set up, even if the pod exists, the service might not have endpoints. But the user mentioned that the service's ENDPOINTS are <none>, but the endpoints command shows an IP. That inconsistency suggests the service isn't properly configured. \n\nWait, the user-service is trying to connect to user-mongodb, but the service might not be correctly pointing to the pod. The service's YAML might be missing the port or targetPort. The LLM previously tried to check the service's YAML and found that the targetPort was missing. They patched it, but the response said no change. Maybe the service was already correctly configured, but the endpoints weren't updated. \n\nAlternatively, the pod might not be running. The user checked the pods in the observe namespace and didn't see user-mongodb. Maybe the pod is in a different namespace or was deleted. The user needs to check all namespaces for the pod. They tried that, but maybe the pod is in a different namespace. \n\nWait, the user mentioned the application belongs to the social-network namespace, but that namespace wasn't found. Maybe the correct namespace is something else, like social-network-ns. The user should check all namespaces again for the pod. \n\nAlternatively, the service might be correctly configured, but the pod isn't running. The user should check the pod's status in all namespaces. If the pod isn't running, they need to investigate why it's not starting. \n\nI think the next step is to check all pods again across all namespaces to confirm the presence of user-mongodb. If it's not there, the issue might be with the deployment or the pod's configuration. If it is there, then the service configuration is the problem. \n\nSo, the LLM should use the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to list all pods again, ensuring they're checking all namespaces. If the pod is missing, they need to check the deployment or recreate the pod. If the pod exists, they should verify the service's configuration to ensure it's correctly pointing to the pod's IP and port.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods --all-namespaces"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-935ee40330028fc4', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 626, 'prompt_tokens': 7835, 'total_tokens': 8461, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfdd2-d5f8-7dd2-a72b-bb0a96369500-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods --all-namespaces'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-935ee40330028fc4', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7835, 'output_tokens': 626, 'total_tokens': 8461}), ToolMessage(content='NAMESPACE      NAME                                                                    READY   STATUS        RESTARTS   AGE\nkube-flannel   kube-flannel-ds-6bfnc